1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,117
A server gets 25 requests at the same moment.

2
00:00:03,277 --> 00:00:05,827
It answers 20 of them and rejects 5.

3
00:00:05,987 --> 00:00:06,755
Why?

4
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:09,390
The code is a token bucket.

5
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:12,100
A token is a permit for one request.

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,793
The bucket holds 20 tokens at most, and it starts full.

7
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:18,584
For each request, the server calls allow().

8
00:00:18,744 --> 00:00:22,541
If the bucket holds a token, allow() takes it and returns True.

9
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:26,822
Thus the first 20 requests of the burst take all 20 tokens.

10
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:29,779
The next 5 requests find an empty bucket.

11
00:00:29,939 --> 00:00:35,132
Then allow() returns False, and the server answers 429, Too Many Requests.

12
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,449
The bucket also earns tokens.

13
00:00:37,609 --> 00:00:39,821
It earns 5 tokens each second.

14
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:42,117
No timer adds these tokens.

15
00:00:42,277 --> 00:00:46,811
At each call, the code multiplies the time since the last call by the rate.

16
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,665
Thus the empty bucket needed 0.2 seconds to earn one token.

17
00:00:51,825 --> 00:00:55,761
The rejected reply gave this time in its Retry-After header.

18
00:00:56,033 --> 00:00:58,850
If no request comes, the bucket fills again.

19
00:00:59,010 --> 00:01:00,953
But it stops at 20 tokens.

20
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:05,754
In a test, a client sent 10 requests each second for 10 seconds.

21
00:01:05,914 --> 00:01:09,246
The bucket allowed 69 of the 100 requests.

22
00:01:09,406 --> 00:01:13,246
20 came from the full bucket, and the others came from the refill.

23
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:16,373
The capacity sets the size of a burst.

24
00:01:16,533 --> 00:01:19,435
The refill rate sets the speed in the long term.

